CAT = "s6Investments"; SEG = "INV"
Q = [
('A fund that returns less than its CAPM expected return for its risk has:',
 'Negative alpha', ['Negative beta', 'Zero beta', 'Positive alpha'],
 'Alpha is the return above or below what the fund\'s risk, measured by beta, predicts. Falling short of the expected return is negative alpha.'),
('A fund with a beta of 1.0 is expected to move:',
 'In line with the market', ['Opposite the market', 'Twice as far as the market', 'Not at all with the market'],
 'Beta measures sensitivity to market moves. A beta of 1.0 means the fund tends to rise and fall about as much as the market does.'),
('The risk-free rate is 2%, the market return is 10% and a fund\'s beta is 0.75. Under CAPM, its expected return is:',
 '8%', ['7.5%', '9.5%', '10%'],
 'CAPM: 2% + 0.75 x (10% - 2%) = 2% + 6% = 8%. Multiplying beta by the full 10% market return gives 7.5%, which forgets the risk-free rate.'),
('Under CAPM, a fund\'s expected return depends on the risk-free rate, the market return and its:',
 'Beta', ['Standard deviation', 'Sales charge', 'Alpha'],
 'CAPM prices only systematic risk, measured by beta. Standard deviation counts total risk, and alpha is the return beyond what CAPM predicts.'),
('An analyst reads the footnotes to a company\'s financial statements mainly because they:',
 'Explain the accounting used', ['Replace the audit report', 'Predict next year\'s price', 'List the stockholders'],
 'Footnotes explain the accounting choices, contingent liabilities and other material details behind the numbers in the statements.'),
('On a balance sheet, total assets minus total liabilities equals:',
 'Shareholders\' equity', ['Net income for the year', 'Total working capital', 'Operating cash flow'],
 'The balance sheet identity is assets = liabilities + equity, so equity, or net worth, is what remains after liabilities.'),
('Which financial statement shows a company\'s revenue and expenses for a period?',
 'Income statement', ['Balance sheet', 'Proxy statement', 'Prospectus cover'],
 'The income statement reports sales, expenses and net income over a period. The balance sheet is a snapshot of assets and liabilities on one date.'),
('When costs are rising, LIFO inventory accounting generally reports:',
 'Lower profits than FIFO', ['Higher profits than FIFO', 'The same profit as FIFO', 'Higher ending inventory'],
 'LIFO charges the newest, costlier inventory to cost of goods sold, so in inflation it shows lower profit and a lower ending inventory than FIFO.'),
('Compared with straight-line, accelerated depreciation makes reported earnings in the early years:',
 'Lower', ['Higher', 'Unchanged', 'Negative by law'],
 'Accelerated methods expense more of an asset\'s cost in the early years, so early earnings are lower and later earnings higher than under straight-line.'),
('Working capital equals:',
 'Current assets minus current liabilities', ['Current liabilities minus current assets', 'Cash plus inventory on hand', 'Total assets minus total liabilities'],
 'Working capital measures short-term liquidity: what is left of current assets after paying current liabilities.'),
('Rule 2a-7 limits a money market fund\'s dollar-weighted average maturity to:',
 '60 days', ['90 days', '120 days', '397 days'],
 'A money market fund\'s weighted average maturity may not exceed 60 days. The 397-day figure is the longest remaining maturity allowed for any one security.'),
('A company earns $4.00 a share and pays $1.20 a share in dividends. Its payout ratio is:',
 '30%', ['3.3%', '33%', '70%'],
 'Payout ratio = dividends per share / earnings per share = $1.20 / $4.00 = 30%. The 70% kept is the retention ratio.'),
('GNMA pass-through securities differ from FNMA securities because GNMA payments are:',
 'Backed by the U.S. government', ['Backed only by the issuing agency', 'Exempt from all taxes', 'Paid only at maturity'],
 'Ginnie Mae is a government agency, so its guarantee carries the full faith and credit of the United States. Fannie Mae is a government-sponsored enterprise without that backing.'),
('A money market fund may generally buy a security only if its remaining maturity is no more than:',
 '397 days', ['60 days', '270 days', '2 years'],
 'Rule 2a-7 caps any single security\'s remaining maturity at 397 calendar days. Sixty days is the limit on the fund\'s weighted average maturity, not on one holding.'),
('A brokered CD sold before maturity may:',
 'Sell below its face value', ['Return its full face value', 'Pay back interest to the bank', 'Lose its FDIC coverage'],
 'Brokered CDs trade in a secondary market, so selling early means taking the market price, which falls when rates rise. FDIC coverage does not protect market value.'),
('A stock dividend paid in additional shares, unlike a cash dividend, is generally:',
 'Not taxed when received', ['Taxed as ordinary income', 'Taxed as a capital gain', 'Taxed as a qualified dividend'],
 'A pro rata stock dividend is usually not taxable when paid. The investor\'s cost basis is spread over the larger number of shares instead.'),
('A participating preferred stock, unlike a straight preferred, may pay its holders:',
 'Extra dividends beyond the stated rate', ['A vote on every board matter', 'A fixed maturity date', 'Interest taxed as ordinary income'],
 'Participating preferred can share in earnings above its stated dividend, usually after common holders receive a set amount. Straight preferred is capped at its stated rate.'),
('A standard listed equity option contract normally covers:',
 '100 shares', ['10 shares', '1,000 shares', '50 shares'],
 'One listed equity option contract covers 100 shares of the underlying stock, adjusted only for certain corporate actions.'),
('Under the conduit theory, a regulated investment company avoids tax on the income it:',
 'Pays out to shareholders', ['Keeps for future buying', 'Earns from munis', 'Reinvests in Treasuries'],
 'A fund that qualifies under Subchapter M is taxed only on income it retains. Income passed through to shareholders is taxed to them instead.'),
('An investor has a $5,000 long-term gain and a $2,000 short-term loss. The net result is a:',
 '$3,000 long-term gain', ['$3,000 short-term gain', '$7,000 long-term gain', '$2,000 short-term loss'],
 'A net short-term loss offsets a net long-term gain: $5,000 - $2,000 leaves a $3,000 net long-term gain, taxed at long-term rates.'),
('Capital gain distributions from a mutual fund are taxed as long-term gains:',
 'Whatever the holding period', ['Only after one full year held', 'Only if reinvested', 'Only if held more than 60 days'],
 'Capital gain distributions reflect the fund\'s own long-term gains, so they are long-term to the shareholder no matter how long the fund shares were held.'),
('For a common stock dividend to be qualified, the shares generally must be held more than 60 days in which window?',
 '121 days around the ex-date', ['30 days before the record date', 'The fund\'s fiscal year', '90 days after payment'],
 'The holding-period test requires more than 60 days of ownership within the 121-day period that starts 60 days before the ex-dividend date.'),
('A fund distribution identified as a return of capital is:',
 'Untaxed, but it lowers basis', ['Taxed as a dividend', 'Taxed as a short-term gain', 'Never shown to the investor'],
 'A return of capital gives back part of the investor\'s own money, so it is not taxed when paid but reduces cost basis, raising the gain at sale.'),
('A fundamental analyst studying a company would focus most on its:',
 'Earnings and balance sheet', ['Price chart patterns over time', 'Daily trading volume', 'Moving averages'],
 'Fundamental analysis judges value from financial statements, earnings, management and industry. Charts, volume and moving averages are technical analysis.'),
]
